detailed_solution:
To compute the mean of $x(n)$, we first express it as a function of $\{w(n), w(n-1), \ldots, w(0)\}$ by iterating the system equation:
\begin{align*}
x(0) &= \alpha x(-1) + w(0) = w(0), \\
x(1) &= \alpha x(0) + w(1) = \alpha w(0) + w(1), \\
&\;\vdots \\
x(n) &= \alpha^n w(0) + \alpha^{n-1} w(1) + \cdots + w(n) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \alpha^k w(n-k).
\end{align*}
Taking the expectation and using the linearity of expectation and the fact that $E\{w(m)\} = \mu_w$ for all $m$, we obtain
\begin{align*}
\mu_x(n) &= E\left\{\sum_{k=0}^{n} \alpha^k w(n-k)\right\} = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \alpha^k E\{w(n-k)\} = \mu_w \sum_{k=0}^{n} \alpha^k.
\end{align*}
Evaluating the geometric series yields
\begin{align*}
\mu_x(n) &= \begin{cases} \mu_w \frac{1 - \alpha^{n+1}}{1 - \alpha}, & \alpha \neq 1 \\ \mu_w (n+1), & \alpha = 1 \end{cases}.
\end{align*}
Since $\mu_x(n)$ is a function of $n$, the process is not stationary. For $|\alpha| < 1$, as $n \to \infty$, $\alpha^{n+1} \to 0$, so $\mu_x(n) \to \frac{\mu_w}{1 - \alpha}$, which is constant. Therefore, the process becomes asymptotically first-order stationary (stationary in the mean) for large $n$ under the stability condition $|\alpha| < 1$.

