Find a $1-\alpha$ confidence interval for $\theta$ based on a single observation $X$ with probability density function:
(a) $f(x|\theta)=1$ for $\theta-\frac{1}{2} < x < \theta+\frac{1}{2}$;
(b) $f(x|\theta)=\frac{2x}{\theta^2}$ for $0 < x < \theta$.

detailed_solution:
There are \(\binom{52}{5}\) equally likely 5-card hands from a standard 52-card deck. To count full houses, first choose the rank that appears three times: \(13\) choices. Then choose a different rank for the pair: \(12\) choices. From the four suits of the triple rank, choose \(3\) cards, giving \(\binom{4}{3}\) choices. From the four suits of the pair rank, choose \(2\) cards, giving \(\binom{4}{2}\) choices. Thus the number of full house hands is \(13\times 12\times \binom{4}{3}\binom{4}{2}\). Therefore the probability is
\[
\frac{13\times 12\times \binom{4}{3}\binom{4}{2}}{\binom{52}{5}}
=\frac{13\times 12\times 4\times 6}{2598960}
=\frac{3744}{2598960}
=\frac{6}{4165}.
\]

