detailed_solution:
This is a classic problem of derangements. Let $A_i$ be the event that soldier $i$ gets their own gun. We want $P(\bigcup_{i=1}^N A_i)$. Using the principle of inclusion-exclusion:

\begin{align*}
P\left(\bigcup_{i=1}^N A_i\right) &= \sum_{i=1}^N P(A_i) - \sum_{i<j} P(A_i \cap A_j) + \sum_{i<j<k} P(A_i \cap A_j \cap A_k) - \cdots + (-1)^{N-1} P(A_1 \cap \cdots \cap A_N) \\
&= \binom{N}{1} \frac{1}{N} - \binom{N}{2} \frac{1}{N(N-1)} + \binom{N}{3} \frac{1}{N(N-1)(N-2)} - \cdots + (-1)^{N-1} \frac{1}{N!} \\
&= \frac{1}{1!} - \frac{1}{2!} + \frac{1}{3!} - \cdots + (-1)^{N-1} \frac{1}{N!} \\
&= \sum_{k=1}^{N} (-1)^{k-1} \frac{1}{k!}
\end{align*}

This is the complement of the probability of a derangement (no one gets their own gun), which is $\sum_{k=0}^{N} \frac{(-1)^k}{k!}$. As $N \to \infty$, the probability approaches $1 - e^{-1} \approx 0.63212$.

